"""Purchase & Warehouse toolkit: purchase orders, goods receipt and quality check, suppliers, multi-location stock,
   ABC analysis and stock valuation, material requisition, and the online purchase order maker.
   Global: workbooks take a currency code on their Settings sheet; the online tool lets the user pick currency and date format."""

KITS = [
    dict(id='purchase', name='Purchase & Warehouse', icon='🏭', color='#4F46E5',
         title='Purchase & Warehouse Toolkit: PO Register, GRN, Supplier Rating and Inventory Templates',
         desc='Free Excel purchase order register with pending quantities, GRN and quality check log, supplier rating, multi-location inventory, ABC analysis and FIFO valuation, plus an online PO maker.',
         intro=['A purchase team and a store room answer the same few questions every day: what have we ordered and not yet received, what arrived and did it pass inspection, which supplier can we rely on, how much of each item is in which godown, and what is all that stock worth.',
                'This toolkit goes deeper than a simple stock register. Its Excel workbooks track every PO line until it is fully received, log each goods receipt with the quantity accepted and rejected, score suppliers on delivery and quality, keep stock across several locations with reorder suggestions, and value stock by FIFO and weighted average with an ABC analysis. A Word material requisition slip and an online purchase order maker complete the paperwork. Pick your own currency; nothing is tied to one country.'],
         who='Purchase executives and managers, store keepers, warehouse in-charges, inventory controllers, factory and project sites, distributors and anyone who buys, receives and issues material.'),
]

ITEMS = [
    dict(id='pw-po-register-excel', kit='purchase', kind='excel', icon='📋', name='Purchase Order Register (Excel)',
         title='Purchase Order Register in Excel: Track Pending PO Quantities and Overdue Deliveries',
         desc='Free Excel PO register: one row per PO line, receipts matched by PO and item, pending quantity and value, overdue lines in red and a pending summary per supplier.',
         files=[('PW-Purchase-Order-Register.xlsx', 'Purchase order register workbook')],
         previews={'PW-Purchase-Order-Register.xlsx': ('Orders', 'A3:Q10')},
         lead='Most purchase follow-up goes wrong in the gap between "we ordered it" and "it has all arrived". This register keeps one row for every line of every purchase order and matches each receipt against it, so it always shows how much is still pending, what that pending material is worth, which lines are past their due date and by how many days. A summary sheet gives each supplier\'s open and overdue position for your weekly follow-up call.',
         inside=['Orders sheet: PO number, PO date, supplier, item, unit, quantity, rate, tax percent and due date for each PO line, 500 ready rows',
                 'Line value, tax and total calculate themselves; received quantity is added up from the Receipts sheet by PO number and item',
                 'Pending quantity and pending value, status (Open, Part received, Overdue, Closed, Short-closed) and days late, with overdue lines highlighted',
                 'Receipts sheet: date, PO number, item, quantity and GRN or challan number, with a check that warns when a receipt does not match any PO line',
                 'Pending by supplier: ordered value, pending value, open lines, overdue lines and the oldest delay for each supplier',
                 'Settings for your company name, currency code and an "as on" date (today unless you type another date)'],
         steps=['On the Settings sheet type your company name and your currency code (for example INR, USD, EUR or AED). Leave "As on" as today.',
                'On the Suppliers sheet replace the sample suppliers with your own. The supplier drop-down on the Orders sheet reads this list.',
                'When a PO is issued, add one row per item on the Orders sheet: PO number, date, supplier, item, unit, quantity, rate, tax percent and the delivery due date.',
                'Each time material arrives, add a row on the Receipts sheet with the date, PO number, the item exactly as written on the PO line and the quantity received. The "Match" column must say OK.',
                'The Orders sheet now shows received and pending quantity for each line. Lines past their due date turn red with the days late.',
                'If a supplier will never send the balance (item discontinued, order cancelled), type Yes in the Short-close column so the line stops showing as pending.',
                'Open the Pending by supplier sheet before your follow-up calls, and filter the Orders sheet by Status = Overdue (Data › Filter) to see what to chase.'],
         tips=['Write the item name the same way on the PO line and on every receipt; copy and paste it if needed. A receipt that says "No such PO line" is not counted anywhere.',
               'For a PO with several delivery dates, enter the item twice with the quantity and due date of each delivery and add -1, -2 to the PO number (PO-104-1, PO-104-2).',
               'Received more than ordered? Received shows the true figure, pending stays at zero and the line closes; discuss the excess with the supplier before accepting it.',
               'Tax percent is only used for the value columns. Leave it 0 if your rates already include tax.'],
         faqs=[('What is a PO register?', 'A list of every purchase order line with what was ordered, what has been received and what is still pending. Purchase teams use it to follow up suppliers and stores use it to know what to expect.'),
               ('How is pending quantity worked out?', 'Quantity ordered minus the total received for that PO number and item on the Receipts sheet, never below zero. A short-closed line has nothing pending.'),
               ('When is a line marked Overdue?', 'When something is still pending and the "As on" date on the Settings sheet is after the line\'s due date. Change the "As on" date to see the position on any day.'),
               ('Can I use it with my accounting or ERP software?', 'Yes. Paste PO lines and receipts exported from your software as values (right-click › Paste Special › Values) into the yellow columns; the grey columns calculate as usual.')]),

    dict(id='pw-grn-qc-excel', kit='purchase', kind='excel', icon='🔍', name='Goods Received Note & Quality Check Log (Excel)',
         title='GRN Format in Excel: Goods Received Note with Quality Check and Rejection Log',
         desc='Free Excel goods received note (GRN) log: challan vs counted vs accepted quantity, shortages and rejections with reasons, a printable GRN and supplier quality summary.',
         files=[('PW-GRN-and-Quality-Check-Log.xlsx', 'GRN and quality check workbook')],
         previews={'PW-GRN-and-Quality-Check-Log.xlsx': ('GRN Log', 'A3:Q9')},
         lead='When a truck arrives, three numbers matter: what the supplier\'s challan or invoice says, what you actually counted, and what passed inspection. This workbook records all three for every item received, works out shortage, excess and rejected quantity, flags lines that need a debit note or replacement, prints a proper goods received note for any GRN number, and sums up each supplier\'s acceptance rate.',
         inside=['GRN Log: GRN number, date, supplier, PO number, challan or invoice number, item, unit, rate, quantity as per challan, quantity received and quantity accepted',
                 'Rejected, shortage and excess quantities, QC result (Accepted, Partly accepted, Rejected, Pending QC), accepted value and an "Action" flag for debit notes',
                 'Rejection reason list: damaged in transit, wrong item, below specification, expired or short shelf life, wrong quantity packed, poor packing, other',
                 'GRN Print sheet: pick a GRN number and get an A4 goods received note with up to 12 lines, totals and signature boxes for stores, quality and approval',
                 'QC Summary: received, accepted and rejected quantity, acceptance percent, shortage and open actions for each supplier, low acceptance in red'],
         steps=['List your suppliers on the Suppliers sheet and type your company name and currency on the Settings sheet.',
                'When goods arrive, give the delivery a GRN number and add one row per item on the GRN Log: date, supplier, PO number, challan number, item, unit, rate and the quantity written on the challan.',
                'Count the goods and type the quantity received. Shortage or excess appears at once.',
                'After inspection, type the quantity accepted. If anything is rejected, pick the reason and type who inspected it. Leave accepted empty while the lot is still under test: it shows "Pending QC".',
                'Open GRN Print, pick the GRN number and print two copies: one for stores and one for accounts with the supplier\'s bill.',
                'Rows marked "Debit note / replacement" need action: send the supplier a debit note or ask for a replacement, and note the reference in your accounts.',
                'Review the QC Summary every month and discuss suppliers below the target acceptance percent.'],
         tips=['Count before you sign the transporter\'s copy. Write "received subject to count and inspection" if you cannot count at once.',
               'Use the same GRN number on every line of one delivery so the print sheet picks them all up.',
               'Accepted quantity is what goes into stock. Post only that into your stock register or the Multi-Location Inventory workbook.',
               'Change the target acceptance percent on the Settings sheet (98 percent to start with) to match your quality policy.'],
         faqs=[('What is a GRN?', 'A goods received note is the store\'s record that material has arrived: from whom, against which PO and challan, how much was received and how much was accepted. Accounts use it to check the supplier\'s bill before payment.'),
               ('What is the difference between shortage and rejection?', 'Shortage is material that never arrived (challan quantity minus counted quantity). Rejection is material that arrived but failed inspection (counted minus accepted). Both reduce what you should pay for.'),
               ('Can one GRN have many items?', 'Yes. Add one row per item with the same GRN number. The print sheet shows up to 12 lines of one GRN.'),
               ('Is the acceptance percent by quantity or value?', 'By quantity: accepted quantity divided by received quantity. Accepted value is shown separately on the log.')]),

    dict(id='pw-supplier-rating-excel', kit='purchase', kind='excel', icon='⭐', name='Supplier Master & Vendor Rating (Excel)',
         title='Vendor Rating Format in Excel: Supplier Master with On-Time, Quality and Lead Time Score',
         desc='Free Excel supplier master and vendor evaluation: contacts, terms and lead times, a delivery log, and an automatic score for on-time, in-full and quality with A, B, C grades.',
         files=[('PW-Supplier-Master-and-Rating.xlsx', 'Supplier master and rating workbook')],
         previews={'PW-Supplier-Master-and-Rating.xlsx': ('Rating', 'A3:L9')},
         lead='Choosing a supplier on price alone is how most delays start. This workbook keeps one clean master list of your suppliers with their contacts, payment terms and promised lead time, logs every delivery against its promised date, and rates each supplier on on-time delivery, delivering in full, quality accepted and your own service score. The weights are yours to set, and the result is a score out of 100 with an A, B or C grade you can share at the review meeting.',
         inside=['Suppliers sheet: code, name, category, contact person, phone, email, city or country, tax ID, payment terms, standard lead time, approval status, service score and last review date',
                 'Deliveries sheet: supplier, PO number, PO date, promised date, received date, quantity ordered, received and accepted; days late, on time, in full, actual lead time and quality percent calculate themselves',
                 'Rating sheet: deliveries, on-time percent, in-full percent, quality percent, average actual lead time against the promised lead time, weighted score, grade and rank',
                 'Settings: weights for on-time, quality, in-full and service, grace days, and the score needed for grade A and B'],
         steps=['On the Settings sheet check the weights (on time 35, quality 35, in full 15, service 15 to start with) and the grace days allowed before a delivery counts as late.',
                'Fill the Suppliers sheet: one row per supplier with its terms and standard lead time in days. Pick the approval status from the list.',
                'Give each supplier a service score from 1 to 5 for things the numbers don\'t show: response to queries, documents on time, help with problems.',
                'For every delivery, add a row on the Deliveries sheet with the PO date, the date promised, the date received and the quantities. Use the GRN log or your stock records for accepted quantity.',
                'Open the Rating sheet. Suppliers with deliveries get a score out of 100, a grade and a rank. Sort by score (Data › Sort) for your review meeting.',
                'Update the Last reviewed date on the Suppliers sheet after you discuss the rating with the supplier.'],
         tips=['Rate on at least five deliveries before you take a decision about a supplier; one late truck says little.',
               'A big gap between the actual and the standard lead time means your reorder levels are too low for that supplier: use the actual figure when planning.',
               'Keep blocked suppliers in the list with status Blocked instead of deleting them, so their history stays.',
               'Share the rating with the supplier. Most improve quickly when they see their own on-time percent.'],
         faqs=[('How is the score calculated?', 'Each measure (on-time percent, quality percent, in-full percent, and the service score divided by 5) is multiplied by its weight, added up and divided by the total of the weights, giving a score out of 100.'),
               ('What counts as on time?', 'A delivery received on or before the promised date plus the grace days on the Settings sheet.'),
               ('What does in full mean?', 'The quantity received is at least the quantity ordered on that delivery row. Part deliveries lower the in-full percent even when they arrive on time.'),
               ('Can I rate service suppliers such as transporters?', 'Yes. Use quantity 1 for each job, and accepted 1 or 0 depending on whether the work was acceptable.')]),

    dict(id='pw-multi-location-excel', kit='purchase', kind='excel', icon='🏬', name='Multi-Location Inventory (Excel)',
         title='Multi-Location Inventory in Excel: Stock by Warehouse with Transfers and Reorder Level',
         desc='Free Excel inventory for up to 8 warehouses, godowns or stores: receipts, issues and transfers in one log, stock by location, reorder alert, suggested order quantity and value.',
         files=[('PW-Multi-Location-Inventory.xlsx', 'Multi-location inventory workbook')],
         previews={'PW-Multi-Location-Inventory.xlsx': ('Stock', 'A3:O9')},
         lead='When stock sits in more than one place (a main warehouse, a factory store, a branch, a site) a single stock figure is not enough. This workbook keeps stock for every item in up to eight locations from one movement log: a receipt has only a "to" location, an issue only a "from" location, and a transfer has both. The Stock sheet shows each location\'s quantity, the company total, the value, and which items have reached their reorder level with a suggested quantity to order.',
         inside=['Locations sheet: up to 8 warehouse, godown, store or site names, plus your currency code',
                 'Items sheet: item code, name, unit, unit cost, reorder level, maximum level and lead time',
                 'Opening sheet: opening stock of each item in each location',
                 'Movements sheet: date, item code, quantity, from location, to location, reference and note; the type (Receipt, Issue, Transfer) and item name fill in by themselves, 2,000 ready rows',
                 'Stock sheet: quantity per location, total, reorder level, status (Reorder, OK, Negative), suggested order quantity up to the maximum level and stock value',
                 'Value by location at the foot of the Stock sheet'],
         steps=['Type your location names on the Locations sheet. They become the column headings everywhere else.',
                'On the Items sheet enter each item with a short unique code, unit, unit cost, reorder level and maximum level.',
                'On the Opening sheet type the stock you hold today in each location.',
                'Record every movement on the Movements sheet. Goods from a supplier: fill only "To". Issue to production or a customer: fill only "From". Moving stock between locations: fill both.',
                'Open the Stock sheet. Each location\'s quantity and the total update as you type. Items at or below the reorder level show "Reorder" with a suggested order quantity.',
                'Count each location once a month and add an adjustment movement (From for a loss, To for a gain) with the note "physical count".'],
         tips=['Use item codes, not names, in the Movements sheet. Codes avoid the "same item, two spellings" problem.',
               'A negative quantity in a location means more was issued or transferred out than was there: check the From location of recent movements.',
               'The reorder level and maximum level are for the whole company. If each location orders on its own, keep one workbook per location instead.',
               'Reorder level = daily use × lead time in days + safety stock is a good starting point.'],
         faqs=[('How does a transfer work?', 'One row with both a From and a To location. It reduces the first location and increases the second by the same quantity, so the company total does not change.'),
               ('Can I have more than 8 locations?', 'The free version has 8 location columns. For more, keep a second file for the other locations or ask for a larger version through the contact page.'),
               ('How is the suggested order quantity worked out?', 'When the total stock is at or below the reorder level, it is the maximum level minus the total stock. Otherwise it is zero.'),
               ('Does it handle batches or expiry dates?', 'Not in this version. Put the batch number in the Note column and filter by it when you need it.')]),

    dict(id='pw-abc-valuation-excel', kit='purchase', kind='excel', icon='🧮', name='ABC Analysis & Stock Valuation, FIFO and Average (Excel)',
         title='ABC Analysis and Inventory Valuation in Excel: FIFO and Weighted Average Cost',
         desc='Free Excel inventory valuation: receipts and issues give closing stock at FIFO and weighted average cost, consumption value, and an ABC classification with a summary chart.',
         files=[('PW-ABC-Analysis-and-Stock-Valuation.xlsx', 'ABC analysis and stock valuation workbook')],
         previews={'PW-ABC-Analysis-and-Stock-Valuation.xlsx': ('ABC', 'A3:I10')},
         lead='Two questions every year end and every stock audit: what is our closing stock worth, and which items deserve the most control? Enter receipts with their cost and issues with their quantity; the workbook works out closing stock for each item, values it by FIFO (first in, first out) and by weighted average cost side by side, and ranks items by consumption value into A, B and C classes so you know where tight control pays off.',
         inside=['Items sheet: item code, name and unit',
                 'Receipts sheet: date, item, quantity, unit cost and reference, with the quantity and value still in stock under FIFO for each receipt lot',
                 'Issues sheet: date, item, quantity and department or job',
                 'Valuation sheet: received, issued and closing quantity, weighted average cost, closing value at average cost, closing value at FIFO and the difference',
                 'ABC sheet: consumption value, share, cumulative share before the item, rank and class, with editable cut-offs (80 and 95 percent to start with)',
                 'ABC summary with number of items and share of value per class, and a bar chart'],
         steps=['On the Settings sheet type your currency code and the period the file covers, and check the A and B cut-off percentages.',
                'List your items on the Items sheet with a unique code.',
                'On the Receipts sheet enter opening stock first (one row per item dated the first day, reference "Opening") and then every purchase in date order with its quantity and unit cost.',
                'On the Issues sheet enter every issue or sale in quantity.',
                'Open the Valuation sheet for closing stock at FIFO and at weighted average cost. Use the method your accountant follows for your books.',
                'Open the ABC sheet. Class A items take most of the consumption value: count them more often, order them more carefully and negotiate their prices first.'],
         tips=['Keep the Receipts sheet sorted by date (Data › Sort, oldest first). FIFO assumes the rows below are the newer lots.',
               'Unit cost should be the landed cost: price plus freight and other costs to bring the goods in, less any tax you can claim back.',
               'Start a new file each year with the closing FIFO lots of the old file as the opening rows.',
               'An item with no issues in the period has zero consumption value and is listed as class C: check whether it is slow-moving or dead stock.'],
         faqs=[('What is ABC analysis?', 'A way to sort stock items by how much money flows through them. Typically a small share of items (class A) makes up about 80 percent of consumption value, the next group (B) about 15 percent, and the many remaining items (C) the last 5 percent.'),
               ('What is the difference between FIFO and weighted average?', 'FIFO assumes the oldest stock is used first, so closing stock is valued at the most recent purchase costs. Weighted average values every unit at the average cost of all units received in the period. When prices rise, FIFO gives a higher closing value.'),
               ('Which method should I use?', 'The one your accounting policy and the accounting standards that apply to you allow, used consistently every year. Ask your accountant; this workbook shows both so you can compare.'),
               ('Why does the average cost here differ from my software\'s moving average?', 'This workbook uses a periodic weighted average over the whole period. Software often uses a moving average that changes after every receipt, which can give a slightly different figure.')]),

    dict(id='pw-material-requisition-word', kit='purchase', kind='word', icon='📝', name='Material Requisition Slip (Word)',
         title='Material Requisition Slip Format in Word: Store Issue Request and Return Note',
         desc='Free editable material requisition slip for Word: department, job or cost centre, items requested and issued, stock check, approvals, and a material return note.',
         files=[('PW-Material-Requisition-Slip.docx', 'Material requisition slip (Word)')],
         lead='Nothing should leave the store without a written request. This material requisition slip (MRS) records who needs what, for which job or cost centre and by when, how much the store actually issued, and what has to be bought because it is not in stock. A short material return note on the second page brings unused material back into the store\'s books.',
         inside=['Requisition number, date, department, cost centre or job number, required-by date and purpose',
                 'Item table: item code, description, unit, quantity requested, quantity issued, balance to be purchased, bin or location and remarks',
                 'Store check: in stock, partly in stock or purchase requisition raised',
                 'Signatures for requested by, approved by (head of department), issued by (store) and received by',
                 'Material return note: return number, original requisition, items returned with condition, and store acknowledgement'],
         steps=['Open the file in Word, Google Docs or LibreOffice and replace [Your Company Name] and the address with your own. Save it as your master copy.',
                'Print a pad of blank slips or fill the slip on screen for each request.',
                'The person needing material fills the top part and the "Quantity requested" column and gets it approved.',
                'The store issues what is available, fills "Quantity issued" and "Balance to purchase", and ticks the store check box.',
                'The person collecting signs "Received by". The store files the slip and posts the issue in its stock records.',
                'When unused material comes back, fill the return note on page two so the stock is added back.'],
         tips=['Number slips in a series per year (MRS/2026/001) and never reuse a number.',
               'Record the job or cost centre on every slip: it is how material cost reaches the right job or department.',
               'For items marked "Balance to purchase", raise a purchase requisition the same day so the request is not forgotten.'],
         faqs=[('What is a material requisition slip?', 'A written request from a department to the store to issue material. It authorises the store to reduce its stock and records which job or department used the material.'),
               ('Is a material requisition the same as a purchase requisition?', 'No. A material requisition asks the store for material it already holds. A purchase requisition asks the purchase department to buy something.'),
               ('Can I add more lines?', 'Yes. Click in the last row of the table and press Tab at the last cell to add a row.')]),

    dict(id='pw-purchase-order-maker', kit='purchase', kind='online', tool='purchase-order', icon='⚡', name='Online Purchase Order Maker',
         title='Online Purchase Order Maker: Free PO with Delivery Terms, Tax and Any Currency',
         desc='Make a purchase order online: supplier, items, discount, tax, freight, delivery terms, payment terms and any currency. Print, save as PDF or download Excel and Word.',
         lead='A clean purchase order in a couple of minutes. Enter your company once, then the supplier, the items with quantity, rate, discount and tax, and the delivery and payment terms. Choose your currency and date format; the PO totals itself and shows the amount in words. Print it, save it as PDF to email, or download it as Excel or Word. Your company details stay saved on this device and the PO number moves to the next one each time.',
         inside=['Your company details, currency (₹, $, €, £, AED and more) and date format, saved on this device',
                 'Supplier name, address, tax ID and quotation reference',
                 'Item lines with code, description, quantity, unit, rate, discount percent and tax percent',
                 'Freight or other charges, tax label of your choice (GST, VAT, Sales tax), totals and amount in words',
                 'Delivery terms (delivered to our site, ex-works, FOB, CIF, DAP, DDP and others), delivery date and address, payment terms and conditions',
                 'Print / PDF, .xlsx and .docx downloads; works offline once the site is installed as an app'],
         steps=['Type your company name, address, tax ID and contact once, and choose your currency and date format. They stay saved on this device.',
                'Enter the PO number, date, supplier details and the supplier\'s quotation reference if any.',
                'Add the items: description, quantity, unit, rate, discount and tax percent. Use "Add item" for more lines.',
                'Choose the delivery terms, type the delivery date, delivery address and payment terms, and add freight if you pay it separately.',
                'Check the preview, then press Print / PDF, or download the PO as Excel or Word.'],
         tips=['Quote the supplier\'s quotation number and date on the PO so both sides refer to the same prices.',
               'Delivery terms decide who pays freight and who carries the risk in transit. If you use Incoterms, write the place after the term (for example FOB Mumbai port).',
               'Record each PO line in the Purchase Order Register workbook so pending deliveries are followed up.',
               'For India, choose ₹ and the tax label GST; the amount in words then uses lakh and crore.'],
         faqs=[('Is my data sent anywhere?', 'No. The PO is built in your browser. Your company details and the last PO number are saved only in this browser on this device.'),
               ('Can I show tax as GST or VAT?', 'Yes. Type the tax label you use; each line has its own tax percent, and the totals show tax by rate.'),
               ('Which currencies can I use?', 'Indian rupee, US dollar, euro, pound sterling, UAE dirham, Saudi riyal, Singapore dollar, Australian dollar, Canadian dollar, South African rand, Nigerian naira, Kenyan shilling, Pakistani rupee, Bangladeshi taka, Sri Lankan rupee, Nepalese rupee and Malaysian ringgit.'),
               ('Is a purchase order a contract?', 'Once the supplier accepts it, a PO is usually treated as the agreed terms of the purchase. For large or unusual purchases, have your terms checked by a professional.')]),
]
